{
"dur": 77.846,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 10.8,
"text": "And some now say that's not just a figure of speech. Several arbitration claims accuse Whatnot of operating, in effect, an unregulated casino, without the safeguards real casinos need.",
"end": 11.075
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 11.075,
"vo_start": 11.35,
"vo_end": 23.03,
"text": "Arbitration is a bit like a private court case, with a hired arbitrator instead of a judge. Whatnot's terms push users into it unless they opt out within thirty days. Whatnot says it's simply faster and cheaper.",
"end": 23.305
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 23.305,
"vo_start": 23.58,
"vo_end": 33.78,
"text": "One lawyer, Paul Lesko, says he represents seventy claimants. He argues that randomised breaks tick every box of an illegal lottery under California law, where Whatnot is based.",
"end": 34.055
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 34.055,
"vo_start": 34.33,
"vo_end": 46.45,
"text": "One: consideration. You pay to get in. Two: a prize. Hosts tell you exactly what the chase card is worth. Three: chance. Which team you get is down to the randomiser.",
"end": 46.725
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 46.725,
"vo_start": 47.0,
"vo_end": 52.996,
"text": "He compares it to paying for a turkey at the supermarket, spinning a wheel at the till, and walking out with broccoli.",
"end": 53.271
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 53.271,
"vo_start": 53.546,
"vo_end": 64.666,
"text": "In July twenty twenty-five, a complaint in California went further, accusing Whatnot and a rival, Fanatics Live, of running unlicensed breaks. But the state didn't prosecute, and there are no criminal charges.",
"end": 64.941
},
{
"i": 7,
"start": 64.941,
"vo_start": 65.216,
"vo_end": 76.896,
"text": "Whatnot rejects all of this. It says gambling isn't allowed on the platform, that rule is strictly enforced, and its randomiser uses open-source code anyone can inspect. The cases are still going on.",
"end": 77.846
}
],
